{"root":{"children":[{"children":[],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"This page is part of my ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":1,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"2026 Boulder Colorado Voter Guide","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://bouldercoloradovoterguide.com/election-guide/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"This page is part of my  for the November 3rd, 2026 General Election in Boulder, Colorado. It covers the Boulder City Council race: the five candidates I am voting for, candidate profiles, other candidates to consider, endorsement comparisons, and additional reporting and sources.","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"You may vote for up to five candidates. Local elected offices are non-partisan, so candidates do not have a party listed next to their name.","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"I am voting for Ryan Schuchard, Jamillah Richmond, Sam Fuqua, Jill Adler Grano, and Tina Marquis.","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Boulder City Council will be electing five people to the council this year (up from the typical four) after the ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"abrupt resignation from Mark Wallach","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/newsletter/%F0%9F%9A%A8-boulder-councilmember-resigns-in-protest-over-airport-vote/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" in August after a vote on the future of the Boulder airport. A total of three incumbents are running for five seats, leading to a strong field of candidates overall.","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"These five candidates bring a strong mix of progressive values, experience on city council, as well as in our school district, while also bringing a new energy and vision to move the city forward (two incumbents and three new members). Here’s a bit more about each:","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Ryan Schuchard","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Ryan has been a leader on transportation, housing, land use and climate since joining council in 2023. He’s been significantly involved in shaping outcomes in ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"adding more housing","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/2025/02/06/in-boulders-latest-zoning-reforms-a-new-way-of-thinking-about-neighborhood-character/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" as well as being a driving force in the Core Arterial Network to ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"make our streets safer","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/2024/09/19/boulder-city-council-allows-iris-avenue-safety-redesign-to-move-forward/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" and more usable for all people. Ryan has pushed forward on ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Boulder’s climate goals","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://www.dailycamera.com/2024/02/26/guest-opinion-ryan-schuchard-colorados-new-climate-roadmap-is-a-resource-for-boulder/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" even when national progress took several steps backward after Republicans won the 2024 election. He’ll continue to be a leader on the next council. ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Ryan Schuchard’s website","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://www.ryanwithboulder.com/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":".","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Jamillah Richmond","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Jamillah is a first-time candidate for city council. I have served with her for the past several years on the Boulder Progressives board. Jamillah has been involved as a chair of Boulder Valley School District’s ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"District Accountability Committee","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://www.bvsd.org/about/district-accountability-committee"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":", as well as running her own business and a non-profit. She also works with ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Street Wise Boulder","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://streetwisearts.org/board-staff"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":", which works with local artists to create unique murals around town. She’s impressed me the speed in which she’s worked hard to build a strong campaign after only deciding to run in early August, gaining a lot of support. She’s authentic and the real deal. ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Jamillah Richmond’s website","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"http://jamillahanewvoice.com/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":".","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Sam Fuqua","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Sam is also a first-time candidate for city council, after previously serving eight years on the Boulder Valley School District Board of Education, serving as president for several years. Sam has lived in Boulder for most of his adult life, often serving the public through a number of ways. He is previously on the ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Boulder Library District board","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nfZuP1uMY4E"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":", and has been involved with public radio through KGNU. Sam is really thoughtful and kind, and I think he’d be a great addition to our city council. ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Sam Fuqua’s website","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://www.fuquaforcouncil.com/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":".","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Jill Adler Grano","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Jill is another non-incumbent that brings a strong background to serving on city council. We both ran together in 2017 and I had supported her that year before she took a role with Rep. Joe Neguse. Since that time, I’ve been impressed with how Jill has continued to deep-dive into Boulder’s housing challenges through her MA at CU and her paper “","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Housing, Capital, and the Contradictions of Progressive Urbanism in Boulder, Colorado","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://d1e1jt2fj4r8r.cloudfront.net/1c85cd7f-8027-44d1-a911-0c364eac7bcf/2d989e98-942e-4dd3-a98b-11a1fec326af/Final-Jill-Grano-Landscapes_of_Privilege_Thesis.pdf"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":".” We also worked together directly on the campaign to move Boulder’s municipal elections to even years (which she chaired), and I’ve been glad to see Jill support a number of progressive causes over the years. ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Jill Adler Grano’s website","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://www.jillforcouncil.com/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":".","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Tina Marquis","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Tina is an incumbent with significant experience as a former president of the BVSD board and now serving on council for the past three years. While I didn’t support Tina in her 2023 run, she’s demonstrated a lot of great qualities as a council person which I think has led to significantly better decisions by council. Her openness to housing and zoning changes, as well as her thoughtfulness on the ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Transportation Maintenance Fee (TMF)","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/2026/01/11/boulder-moves-toward-billing-residents-and-businesses-for-new-transportation-maintenance-fee/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" and ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":8,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Downtown Development Authority (DDA)","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":null,"target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/2026/08/02/boulder-city-council-divided-over-whether-to-put-downtown-tax-measure-on-november-ballot/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" have been significant. Tina always approaches our city’s problems with an open mind and then works hard to find the best path forward. ","type":"extended-text","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Tina Marquis’ website","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer","target":null,"title":null,"url":"https://www.tinaforboulder.com/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":".","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Other candidates you may consider","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"I feel strongly about my support for the candidates above, whom I believe are the strongest progressive candidates in the race, although I do want to write a bit more about other candidates I have also considered this year.","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Rachel Rose Isaacson","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Rachel is running in 2026 after a first run last year. I think Rachel is generally aligned with progressive positions. Like both Jamillah and Sam, she lives in permanently affordable housing which is a needed voice on our city council. Rachel’s experience and support for working people is another plus. Given this year’s strong field of candidates, I would still like to see Rachel continue to build depth on city policy and management.","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Tara Winer","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Tara is the third incumbent running for city council this year. Tara is running for her third term after first getting elected in 2021. While I wouldn’t label Tara as a progressive based on a number of her positions and voting record (particularly on topics around policing, minimum wage, and related issues), I do think Tara has made good contributions on city council to support more housing and being an influential voice for improving transportation in Boulder. I appreciate Tara personally and believe she is a solid council person amongst a number of strong candidates this year.","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Comparing endorsements in Boulder's city council race","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Here's a helpful diagram of the known endorsements in Boulder's 2026 city council race. Note that I have only listed candidates which have received at least one endorsement or recommendation from an established city group or organization.","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"paragraph","version":1},{"type":"image","version":1,"src":"https://bouldercoloradovoterguide.com/content/images/2026/10/2026-Boulder-Council-Endorsements-Graphic.png","width":1900,"height":2000,"title":"","alt":"A comparison of endorsements in Boulder's 2026 city council race from Boulder Colorado Voter Guide","caption":"<span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">A comparison of endorsements in Boulder's 2026 city council race from Boulder Colorado Voter Guide</span>","cardWidth":"regular","href":""},{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Additional Reporting, Commentary, and Sources","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"extended-heading","version":1,"tag":"h2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"2026 Council Candidate Listings","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://bouldercolorado.gov/2026-city-boulder-mayoral-and-city-council-candidates"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — City of Boulder (official)","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":1},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"2026 Boulder City Council race coverage","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/tag/boulder-city-council/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Boulder Reporting Lab","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":2},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Meet the 19 candidates running for Boulder mayor and City Council in 2026","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/2026/08/30/meet-the-19-candidates-running-for-boulder-mayor-and-city-council-in-2026/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Boulder Reporting Lab","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":3},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Sam Fuqua running for Boulder City Council","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://www.dailycamera.com/2026/09/04/sam-fuqua-boulder-city-council/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Daily Camera","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":4},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Fuqua: Pay His Family's Boulder Opportunities Forward","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://richardvalenty.com/2026/09/fuqua-pay-his-familys-boulder-opportunities-forward/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Richard Valenty","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":5},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Sam Fuqua candidate profile","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/govpack_profiles/sam-fuqua/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Boulder Reporting Lab","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":6},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Grano: Re-localize Boulder's Economy","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://richardvalenty.com/2026/09/grano-re-localize-boulders-economy/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Richard Valenty","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":7},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Jill Adler Grano candidate profile","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/govpack_profiles/jill-grano/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Boulder Reporting Lab","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":8},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Isaacson: Can Working Class Boulderites Stay Here?","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://richardvalenty.com/2026/09/isaacson-can-working-class-boulderites-stay-here/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Richard Valenty","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":9},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Rachel Rose Isaacson candidate profile","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://boulderreportinglab.org/govpack_profiles/rachel-rose-isaacson-2/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Boulder Reporting Lab","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":10},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Marquis: Can Boulder Housing Be Family-Friendly?","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"link","version":1,"rel":"noreferrer noopener","target":"_blank","title":null,"url":"https://richardvalenty.com/2026/09/marquis-can-boulder-housing-be-family-friendly/"},{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":" — Richard Valenty","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"listitem","version":1,"value":11},{"children":[{"children":[{"detail":0,"format":0,"mode":"normal","style":"","text":"Tina Marquis candidate profile","type":"extended-text","version":1}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"lin